{
  "result": "The first thing the town librarian did when the power went out was open the blinds. It was four in the afternoon, and there was still enough light to read by, though not enough to pretend the windows were merely decorative.\n\nAcross the street, a baker carried a tray of unbaked rolls onto the sidewalk. He stood there for a moment, apparently expecting the weather to offer a second opinion. Then he carried them back inside.\n\nIn the children\u2019s room, a boy asked whether the books would still work. His grandmother said yes, then looked down at the phone in her hand as if she had just been caught asking the same question.\n\nBy five, people had begun to bring their chairs closer to the windows. This made the library look less like a library and more like a bus traveling very slowly toward evening. The librarian found herself whispering the names of streets as she passed between the tables. Nobody heard her except a woman doing a crossword, who looked up and said, \u201cNot yet. I get off at Maple.\u201d\n\nWhen the lights came back, the refrigerator in the staff room gave its usual irritated click. Someone cheered. Across the street, the baker slid his rolls into the oven. The librarian reached for the cord to close the blinds, then stopped. On the other side of the glass, the woman with the crossword was walking home. At the corner of Maple, she turned and waved with her yellow pencil.",
